WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
- A few years ago, my next
guests were just two dudes

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.770
going on a surfing trip to Bali,

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.514
which is awesome, sounds fun.

00:00:07.514 --> 00:00:10.240
It was an adventure that
change their lives, though.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.600
Now it's changing the fate of the planet.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.570
Please welcome from 4Ocean,
Andrew Cooper and Alex Schulze,

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:17.732
today's Rad Human.

00:00:17.732 --> 00:00:19.285
(crowd cheering)

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:22.368
(rock music playing)

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.160
That's our Rad Humans,
that's what that is.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.925
(laughing)

00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:30.590
- [Kelly] Explained just for you.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.410
Explain what 4Ocean is.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:33.800
- [Alex] So 4Ocean's an
ocean cleanup company

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.710
that Andrew and I started
about three years ago.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.350
We were on a surfing
trip in Bali, Indonesia,

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.830
saw crazy amounts of
plastic on the coastline

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:41.663
and in the ocean,

00:00:41.663 --> 00:00:42.740
- [Alex] And we wanted
to do something about it.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.560
So we started by selling a bracelet

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.420
made from recycled materials
and for every one sold,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.300
we'd remove a pound of trash in the ocean.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.660
- That's amazing! So why bracelets?

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.240
- So we wanted to is give
anyone anywhere in the world

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.080
the opportunity to clean the ocean,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.460
regardless of how far they
live from a coastline.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.960
What bracelets do is that it's
pretty much gender-neutral

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.340
and it goes with any outfit.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.240
It's also that reminder
that stays with you

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.280
for every action or decision you make.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.113
Just look down at your bracelet

00:01:06.113 --> 00:01:07.260
and you can make that change.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.820
- [Kelly] That's really cool!
So cleaning up our oceans

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.425
is important to you both as
well, right? Alfre and...

00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:14.104
- Yes, definitely.
- One hundred percent.

00:01:14.104 --> 00:01:15.343
- I mean, I'm Aquaman.

00:01:15.343 --> 00:01:19.093
(crowd cheers)
- That's right

00:01:21.723 --> 00:01:25.213
I mean, it's kind of a no-brainer to go.

00:01:25.213 --> 00:01:28.520
- [Kelly] Kind of my thing,
I don't know if you know.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.500
How many pounds of plastic have
you recovered and recycled?

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.590
- [Alex] So we're stoked to say our teams

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.490
are working incredibly
hard seven days a week,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.420
just recently passed our
seven millionth pound of trash

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:38.253
removed from the ocean.

00:01:38.253 --> 00:01:41.522
(crowd cheers)

00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:42.355
- [Kelly] I mean yes, but
think like, oh my god,

00:01:42.355 --> 00:01:44.860
- [Kelly] I mean yes, but
think like, oh my god,

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.840
there's that much trash in the ocean.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:47.673
- [Andrew] Oh yeah.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:48.506
- And there's still so much to go

00:01:48.506 --> 00:01:50.219
and that's so much trash, right?

00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:53.940
Who does that? Who just just
throws stuff out in the ocean?

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:54.980
Who are you?

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.530
I don't understand who does that?

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.600
- [Andrew] That's actually a
really good point to bring up

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.670
and it's not a mysterious ship that's just

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.930
cruising our shore
throwing the plastic over.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.780
What we're finding is that all the plastic

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.010
that we're collecting
from our beach cleanups

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.460
and offshore on the boat is
that it's coming from land.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.570
So it's land-based sources.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.520
It's plastic that people
are using everyday

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.090
and it's not getting discarded properly

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.810
and it ends up back in the ocean so

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.560
one of our biggest messages is like

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.380
we gotta stop it at the
source and stop it on land.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.710
- [Kelly] Amen. Yes, I'm in.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.270
So I feel like us humans sometimes,

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.440
unless it directly impacts
us on a daily basis

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.480
it's just out of sight, out of mind,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.814
and it's gonna directly
impact us at some point.

00:02:32.814 --> 00:02:35.490
I mean it already is, like open your eyes.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.240
It's such an important
thing that y'all are doing

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.530
and I'm so thankful that y'all are here so

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:40.590
you have products as well

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:41.808
that are more than bracelets, right?

00:02:41.808 --> 00:02:43.115
- [Alex] Yes, absolutely.
- [Andrew] That's right.

00:02:43.115 --> 00:02:45.890
- And people can buy these and that money

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:47.080
goes towards helping, right?

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.960
- [Alex] Yes, yes. Absolutely.
So every product purchase,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.770
we remove a pound of trash from the ocean.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:51.603
Yep, so we're coming out.

00:02:51.603 --> 00:02:53.050
So we started with a
bracelet that was kind of

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.100
our first stepping stone into,
you know, starting 4Ocean.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.950
And now we're offering different tools

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.480
to reducing plastic consumption.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.530
So reusable mugs, reusable
bottles, shopping bags, cutlery,

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.450
basically any item to reduce
single-use plastic consumption.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.250
- That's really cool! (crowd cheers)

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.520
So beyond buying 4Ocean
products, how can people help?

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:13.547
If people are at home right now going,

00:03:13.547 --> 00:03:14.380
"I'm not going to the ocean,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.960
I'm not doing this, like, I'll recycle."

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.320
Like what can they really do?

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.240
- [Alex] So we kinda look at
that as three different ways

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.420
that people can help the
ocean plastic crisis.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.790
One is reduce and eliminate

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.080
your single-use plastic consumption.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.040
So bring that bottle,
bringing that shopping bag,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.010
bringing that different tool
so you don't have to continue

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:32.000
to purchase all these items
wrapped in single-use plastic.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:32.870
That's one way.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.720
Number two is keep the
conversation going, right?

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.500
All these posts on social media,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.990
everybody having a conversation,

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.870
it's an opportunity to raise awareness

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.230
about ocean plastic and
exactly what's happening

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.550
and keep that conversation going.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.270
And number three is, we need
to hold brands accountable.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.310
Right? If we could come up
with sustainable alternatives

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.630
to that single-use plastic,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:51.510
then we can come up with a solution.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.300
Like Jason right here,
he's got the Mananalu

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.390
which is offering water in aluminum cans

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.060
and giving people the
option to use aluminum

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.750
which is 100% recyclable, over plastic.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.270
So it's little things like that.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.079
- This is you, right?
This is your new line.

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:04.912
- [Jason] Yeah.

00:04:04.912 --> 00:04:05.745
- Yeah. What made you start this? Yeah.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:08.960
- Yeah. What made you start this? Yeah.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.420
- Because, I mean, I lived
the single-use plastic life.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.080
I mean I'm constantly on an airplane,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.340
I'm constantly in a hotel
traveling, you know,

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:17.750
on the airplane we'll see, you know,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.440
there's a Coca-Cola
can, there's a beer can,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:20.865
there's sparkling water,
and then there's this

00:04:20.865 --> 00:04:24.457
goddamn plastic bottle
that's like a shot of plastic

00:04:24.457 --> 00:04:25.810
that's three different types:

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.690
the wrapper, the bottle, then the cap.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.507
It is not recyclable at all.

00:04:29.507 --> 00:04:30.340
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.530
- Absolutely going into a landfill

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.350
or it's going into the
ocean, like just ban it.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.000
And the plastic itself
going into your body?

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.023
- [Kelly] That's what I was about to say.

00:04:37.023 --> 00:04:38.025
- [Jason] It's not even good for you

00:04:38.025 --> 00:04:38.858
- I just had blood work done

00:04:38.858 --> 00:04:43.050
and I have so much plastics
in my blood. You have no idea.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.080
It is directly impacting you.
How much plastic is around is

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.670
and how it seeps into us, like.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.470
- [Jason] Just this of it
lies in the top of the ocean

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.740
makes them like 15% of what
you're actually saying.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.260
Because the amount of
nano-tech, I mean like,

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.062
it's gonna blow your mind
and if you do the research.

00:04:55.062 --> 00:04:55.895
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.740
- So nano-plastics. But
just on the surface with,

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.310
I'm trying to do and these
gentlemen are trying to do

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:03.060
just to collect that,
is a whole wave change.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.220
We just have to rethink
everything that we're doing

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.053
and be conscious of it.

00:05:06.053 --> 00:05:07.180
Sometimes it awesome that you have that

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.010
to constantly remind yourself but like,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.892
we've had things that
can do to change, I mean.

00:05:10.892 --> 00:05:13.340
Bring your water bottle,
fill it up, drink from home,

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:14.720
have you bring a tap,
whatever you want to.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.920
But when you go out and
it's still getting made.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.110
So even if I do wanna live the clean life,

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.100
seeing those plastic
bottles are still made.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.105
And so I wanna be able to make a change.

00:05:24.105 --> 00:05:25.593
- [Kelly] And it's a pint of
can. If you don't demand...

00:05:25.593 --> 00:05:27.620
- [Jason] Yeah, but be able to
go to these bigger companies

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.310
and go, you need to change.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.980
I want an option, there's no options.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.930
And so I made Mananalu
because it's the way

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.794
and then I'm Aquaman as
well, I gotta do something.

00:05:35.794 --> 00:05:37.627
- That is so smart. So smart.

00:05:37.627 --> 00:05:38.717
(crowd cheers)

00:05:38.717 --> 00:05:41.600
So audience these rad
humans want you to be a part

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.030
of this global movement as well.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:45.080
So they're hooking each one of you up

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.250
with a 4Ocean reusable gift bag.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.990
There's a hat, bracelet,
reusable water bottle,

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.790
a T-shirt, and a metal straw

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.140
so you can also reducing
how much plastic you see,

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.580
how much plastic we use.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.983
This is such a cool thing. I'm so in.

